  "metadata": {
    "title": "Why investors can't and shouldn't ignore the AI trade",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Yahoo Finance Breaking News Reporter Jake Conley and Opening Media CEO Phil Rosen join Julie Hyman on Morning Brief to discuss why the AI trade has become a major driver of market performance and why they believe it could continue to fuel gains.\nI would argue that the thing that's working best in the economy is AI and that's the thing that's driving markets. That's the only theme people want exposure to right now and that's been several years of that.\nAnd you see FOMO from investors on wanting to not wanting to miss out on the AI trade.\nYeah. Of course. And if you have been sidelined through the whole AI rally, you're losing a lot of money and if you're trying to play other corners of the market that don't have exposure to AI, one that's getting increasingly difficult to do because AI is touching everything, but two, you're also losing money there.\nSo the uh the bearishness, I totally hear it, but I think it\nthe risk reward just isn't there to get bearish right now in in my view. And uh, look, if you look at tech stocks today versus even like six, seven months ago, in many cases they're cheaper because earnings have gotten so good and price action hasn't actually kept up with earnings in many cases and I remember a year ago,\npeople were saying Micron looks stretched when it was up 40% and it's up 1,000% now\nsince those levels. So to me it's like, okay, do you really want to pull out when we've seen a little bit of a rally when maybe we're still in uh, you know, as Dan Ive says, whatever third inning of a nine inning uh AI boom.\nWell, and and I think the other question is, does it have to be absolutist, like either you're in or you're out, right?\nYeah, that's not really the questions more like, do you take out protection somewhere? Do you hedge somewhere? Um, but I think the answer to that is like, how do you do that? It's kind of, you know, this market is a little bit\ntricky to\nIt's hard to hide from from the AI trade and I I think even if people say, look, you want to diversify globally. Well, people do that by going to Asia mostly and Southeast Asia, but that that's all AI as well. So if you, you know, if you say I want to get exposure in Southeast Asia, well that's all Taiwan semi, SK Hynx, Samsung anyway. So it's not even diversifying there. So I think, yes, there's a risk of concentration and theme, but because the theme has been so resilient and pretty much ignored every single bearish headwind in the last three years.\nI don't know if it's uh worth giving credence to the bearish takes right now. I know BV is very smart, a lot of smart people at these firms.\nTo me, look, they got to come out at some point with these bearish calls if their coverage continues to be 80% buy ratings anyway.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Yahoo Finance Breaking News Reporter Jake Conley and Opening Media CEO Phil Rosen join Julie Hyman on Morning Brief to discuss why the AI trade has become a major driver of market performance and why they believe it could continue to fuel gains.",
      "I would argue that the thing that's working best in the economy is AI and that's the thing that's driving markets. That's the only theme people want exposure to right now and that's been several years of that.",
      "And you see FOMO from investors on wanting to not wanting to miss out on the AI trade.",
      "Yeah. Of course. And if you have been sidelined through the whole AI rally, you're losing a lot of money and if you're trying to play other corners of the market that don't have exposure to AI, one that's getting increasingly difficult to do because AI is touching everything, but two, you're also losing money there.",
      "So the uh the bearishness, I totally hear it, but I think it",
      "the risk reward just isn't there to get bearish right now in in my view. And uh, look, if you look at tech stocks today versus even like six, seven months ago, in many cases they're cheaper because earnings have gotten so good and price action hasn't actually kept up with earnings in many cases and I remember a year ago,",
      "people were saying Micron looks stretched when it was up 40% and it's up 1,000% now",
      "since those levels. So to me it's like, okay, do you really want to pull out when we've seen a little bit of a rally when maybe we're still in uh, you know, as Dan Ive says, whatever third inning of a nine inning uh AI boom.",
      "Well, and and I think the other question is, does it have to be absolutist, like either you're in or you're out, right?",
      "Yeah, that's not really the questions more like, do you take out protection somewhere? Do you hedge somewhere? Um, but I think the answer to that is like, how do you do that? It's kind of, you know, this market is a little bit",
      "tricky to",
      "It's hard to hide from from the AI trade and I I think even if people say, look, you want to diversify globally. Well, people do that by going to Asia mostly and Southeast Asia, but that that's all AI as well. So if you, you know, if you say I want to get exposure in Southeast Asia, well that's all Taiwan semi, SK Hynx, Samsung anyway. So it's not even diversifying there. So I think, yes, there's a risk of concentration and theme, but because the theme has been so resilient and pretty much ignored every single bearish headwind in the last three years.",
      "I don't know if it's uh worth giving credence to the bearish takes right now. I know BV is very smart, a lot of smart people at these firms.",
      "To me, look, they got to come out at some point with these bearish calls if their coverage continues to be 80% buy ratings anyway."
